<Base title="Human-Directed Software Engineering"><section class="wrap pagehero"><div class="eyebrow">An educational approach in development</div><h1>Human-Directed Software Engineering.</h1><p>AI can help produce code. The engineer still has to understand the problem, make decisions, and verify the result.</p></section><section class="wrap section" style="padding-top:0"><div class="bodycopy"><p>Human-Directed Software Engineering (HDSE) is an approach I'm developing for teaching and practicing software engineering with AI-assisted tools. It treats the human engineer as responsible for requirements, architecture, verification, and the final product.</p><h2>Four working principles</h2><div class="listcards"><article class="listcard"><h3>Understand before accepting</h3><p>Students should be able to explain what their software does and why it works, regardless of how the code was produced.</p></article><article class="listcard"><h3>Make work verifiable</h3><p>Use tests, documentation, small changes, and reviewable artifacts so claims about correctness can be checked.</p></article><article class="listcard"><h3>Keep the project authoritative</h3><p>Requirements, decisions, source code, and tests should live in the project—not only in an AI chat session.</p></article><article class="listcard"><h3>Use AI deliberately</h3><p>Choose when assistance helps learning and engineering, and when independent work is necessary to build understanding.</p></article></div><h2>Where this is going</h2><p>I'm exploring lessons, exercises, and open educational materials that put these principles into practice. HDSE is a developing framework, not a completed degree program or an official college curriculum.</p><p><a class="textlink" href="/teaching">My teaching interests</a></p></div></section></Base>
